const TITLE = "About – How The Long Weekends Works | The Long Weekends";
const DESCRIPTION =
  "How The Long Weekends plans your 2026 and 2027 leave: where the holiday dates come from, how the solver picks the cheapest long break, and what it will not do.";
---

<DocPage
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/about"
  eyebrow="About"
  heading="Why this site exists"
  lede="Public holidays are fixed. Your leave balance is not. This tool lines the two up and tells you the cheapest way to buy a long break."
  updated="2026-08-24"
>
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    Most holiday calendars show you a grid of dates and stop there. The useful
    question is not <em>when is Diwali</em> — it is <strong>which leave days are
    worth spending</strong>, and that depends on which weekday each holiday lands
    on.
  </p>
  <p>
    2026 has <strong>nine long weekends that cost no leave at all</strong>, because
    the holiday falls on a Friday or a Monday. Spend a single leave day and <strong>22 four-day breaks</strong> open up across 13 different festivals. The
    calculator finds these for you and sorts them by how soon they arrive, so you
    can book the next one rather than reading a list of dates that have passed.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the dates come from</h2>
  <p>
    Holiday dates are transcribed from official notifications, not aggregated from
    other calendar sites:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the <strong>Department of Personnel and Training (DoPT)</strong> gazetted
      holiday list for central government offices
    </li>
    <li>
      the <strong>Reserve Bank of India</strong> list under section 25 of the
      Negotiable Instruments Act, which is what banks actually close for
    </li>
    <li>
      state government gazettes for the regional lists — currently Karnataka,
      Maharashtra, Delhi, West Bengal and Tamil Nadu
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Several Indian festivals are set by lunar observation and a few state
    notifications land only weeks ahead, so a date can move after publication. If
    you spot one that has, <a href="/contact">tell us</a> — it is the single most
    useful thing you can report.
  </p>

  <h2>How the maths works</h2>
  <p>
    For a given number of leave days, the solver walks the calendar looking for
    runs of consecutive days off, treating your weekend, the public holidays for
    your region, and the leave you are willing to spend as one continuous block.
    Two rules keep the results honest:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every plan must contain a real holiday.</strong> Taking Friday and
      Monday off around an ordinary weekend is not a long weekend, it is four days
      of leave.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A plan must return at least 1.3 days off per leave day spent.</strong> Below that you are not bridging a gap, you are just taking leave, and the
      tool has nothing to add.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Results are then ranked by <strong>how soon they start</strong>, not by how
    long they are. A spectacular 18-day break next March is no use to someone
    deciding what to do in three weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>Six-day work weeks</h2>
  <p>
    A large share of Indian workplaces run Monday to Saturday, which changes every
    answer on the page — a Saturday holiday is a real day off if you normally work
    it, and Friday holidays stop producing three-day weekends. The work-week switch
    in the header re-solves the whole calendar for a six-day week rather than
    adjusting the display.
  </p>

  <h2>Your office holiday list</h2>
  <p>
    Most companies publish their own list, which overlaps the gazette but is never
    identical. The <a href="/company-optimizer">office holiday optimizer</a> takes
    that list — pasted as text, or as a PDF or image — reads the dates out of it,
    and runs the same solver against your actual holidays.
  </p>
  <p>
    The parsing happens <strong>in your browser</strong>. Your company's holiday
    list is not uploaded anywhere, which is both a privacy property and the reason
    the feature works without an account. There is more detail in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not do</h2>
  <p>
    Worth knowing before you rely on it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It covers <strong>2026 only</strong>, plus the first days of January 2027 so
      that a New Year bridge can be calculated.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Restricted or optional holidays</strong> are not modelled. Those vary
      per employer and are usually the interesting ones, which is what the office
      optimizer is for.
    </li>
    <li>
      It does not know your leave balance, your team's blackout dates, or whether
      your manager will approve anything.
    </li>
    <li>
      It is not a booking site and has no commercial relationship with any airline
      or hotel.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who runs it</h2>
  <p>
    An independent project, built and maintained by one person in India. There is
    no company behind it, no sign-up, no newsletter and no advertising. If it is
    useful, the best thanks is to send a correction when a date is wrong — the
    address is on the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</DocPage>
